WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.431
Process theories ask how people decide where to put effort.

2
00:00:03.631 --> 00:00:06.696
Vroom's expectancy theory connects three perceptions.

3
00:00:06.896 --> 00:00:10.647
Expectancy asks whether effort can produce the needed performance.

4
00:00:10.847 --> 00:00:14.656
Instrumentality asks whether that performance will lead to an outcome.

5
00:00:14.856 --> 00:00:18.018
Valence asks how much the person values that outcome.

6
00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:23.333
The model multiplies these factors, so one missing link can undermine the whole connection.

7
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:29.630
Imagine an employee who wants to learn audio editing but has neither working software nor practice time.

8
00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:33.907
Promising more recognition does not repair the effort-to-performance link.

9
00:00:34.107 --> 00:00:37.711
Clear instructions, equipment, and supported practice might help.

10
00:00:37.911 --> 00:00:42.392
Even with those in place, a promised opportunity must be credible and valued.

11
00:00:42.592 --> 00:00:49.785
Equity theory adds a different question: how does someone judge their contributions and outcomes compared with a relevant other?

12
00:00:49.985 --> 00:00:55.239
Perceived unfairness can matter even when a manager believes the arrangement is reasonable.

13
00:00:55.439 --> 00:00:58.496
Investigate the comparison and the circumstances.

14
00:00:58.696 --> 00:01:04.720
Neither formula nor comparison lets us calculate a colleague's actual motivation from a distance.
